ROLE: evaluation_prosecutor  VOTE: yellow
MANDATORY_BASELINES: [{"name": "Global CategoricalDtype", "why": "Standard method to align categorical schemas across partitioned pandas dataframes", "repo_or_cite": "pandas.CategoricalDtype docs / Dask Categorical docs"}, {"name": "sklearn.preprocessing.OrdinalEncoder", "why": "Standard ML pipeline method for stable string-to-integer mappings across training and serving datasets", "repo_or_cite": "scikit-learn docs"}]
STRAWMAN_RISKS: ["The multi-shard pipeline is a strawman: it extracts raw .cat.codes from independently cast local categoricals without establishing a global vocabulary, ignoring standard multi-file ML preprocessing practices.", "Comparing a single-file pipeline (which implicitly has global visibility of all categories) to a multi-file pipeline without adding a global encoder creates an artificial failure."]
MISSING_METRICS: ["Model performance (R^2) when standard global vocabulary controls are applied (to differentiate between a framework coupling and a missing-component user error)"]
KILLER_EXPERIMENT: Re-run the partial-observation multi-shard sweep, but instead of using a plain `astype("category")`, cast the column using a predefined schema: `astype(pd.CategoricalDtype(categories=global_vocab_list))` or process the raw strings with a globally fitted `OrdinalEncoder`. If the R^2 collapse goes to 0, it proves the failure is merely the expected outcome of omitting standard schema management, rather than a novel framework defect.
RATIONALE: The evaluation relies on a strawman pipeline. It is a well-known requirement in distributed data processing (explicitly documented by parallel pandas frameworks like Dask) that categorical columns must share a known, global vocabulary to have stable underlying integer codes. Extracting local `.cat.codes` across independent shards without an encoder or a shared `CategoricalDtype` guarantees a mismatched integer contract. The observed collapse is an artifact of skipping standard baseline preprocessing.
